EN = [
 ('dig1', 'The brush uncovers a temple that is below the ground around it. Builders start at the bottom and go up.'),
 ('dig2', 'Whoever made this started at the top of a hillside and cut downward, removing the rock around the temple until the building was left standing in a courtyard of its own. Nothing was added. Everything was taken away.'),
 ('src1', "Inscriptions, which for this period are the backbone — copper plates and temple inscriptions from all three powers. Travellers' accounts, including Arab merchants and geographers writing about western India. And texts, including a Sanskrit poem that is one of our better sources for the politics."),
 ('frz1', 'Three powers, and one city. The Palas in the east, in Bengal and Bihar. The Gurjara-Pratiharas in the west, in Rajasthan and the Gangetic west. The Rashtrakutas in the Deccan.'),
 ('frz2', "For roughly two centuries each of them tried to take Kannauj — Harsha's old capital, now the symbol of paramountcy over northern India. Hold the city and you were the pre-eminent power. That is the tripartite struggle."),
 ('frz3', "Each is worth knowing for more than the fighting. The Palas, founded by Gopala and strongest under Dharmapala and Devapala, were Buddhism's last great royal patrons in India: they endowed Nalanda and founded Vikramashila, and Buddhist scholars went from their courts to Tibet."),
 ('frz4', 'The Pratiharas held the west for a long time and are credited with resisting Arab expansion beyond Sindh. The Rashtrakutas, under rulers including Dhruva, Govinda the Third and Amoghavarsha, were the most frequently successful of the three in the north, and the most interesting at home.'),
 ('frz5', 'And the Rashtrakutas built Kailasa at Ellora, which is the monument of this episode. Cut downward out of the hillside in the eighth century under Krishna the First, it is a complete temple — tower, hall, gateway, courtyard — excavated rather than constructed.'),
 ('frz6', 'Ellora also holds Buddhist, Jain and Hindu caves side by side on the same hillside, which is worth noticing: three traditions patronised at one site.'),
 ('frz7', 'So how does it end? Nobody wins. Kannauj changes hands repeatedly; the Rashtrakutas raid north and withdraw south; the Pratiharas hold it longest and then fragment; the Palas decline and revive and decline again.'),
 ('frz8', "By the tenth century all three are weakening and their own feudatories are becoming independent kingdoms — the Rajput houses in the west, the Chandelas, the Paramaras, and in the Deccan the Chalukyas of Kalyani. The struggle's real outcome is not a winner but a map of regional states."),
 ('plq1', 'A caution about how this is often told. The common account says the three wore each other out, left the north divided, and so prepared the way for later invasions. There is something in it.'),
 ('plq2', "But the more careful reading points out that each power declined for reasons of its own — succession disputes, pressure from feudatories, regional revolts — that the period produced major temples, literature and learning rather than decay, and that treating two centuries of history as a prelude to someone else's arrival is a poor way to understand it. Use the narrative; do not let it do your thinking."),
 ('ins1', 'Here is the memory tip, carved in stone. Pala east, Pratihara west, Rashtrakuta south — all three for Kannauj.'),
 ('quiz1', 'Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. First: the city all three powers fought to control. Kannauj.'),
 ('quiz2', 'Second: the monastery founded by the Palas, alongside their patronage of Nalanda. Vikramashila. Third: the temple at Ellora cut downward out of the hillside under the Rashtrakutas.'),
 ('quiz3', 'Kailasa. In Mains, a standard question asks about the consequences of the tripartite struggle.'),
 ('quiz4', 'Give the conventional answer, then complicate it: regional states formed, and the period was culturally productive, not simply a decline.'),
 ('gal1', 'Kailasa takes its place in the gallery, and the sixth room is full: a temple made by removing a hillside.'),
 ('gal2', "Next gallery: those regional states in their own right — not as the end of something, but as what came next. To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios."),
]

